1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,386
This is Unmask.

2
00:00:01,386 --> 00:00:03,803
The landing page makes the promise:

3
00:00:03,803 --> 00:00:06,428
bid blind, win fair, check everything.

4
00:00:06,428 --> 00:00:10,158
And right below, the same eleven bids on two launches:

5
00:00:10,158 --> 00:00:14,440
bots win a first-come launch, and win nothing on a sealed one.

6
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:16,706
Next, the launches list.

7
00:00:16,706 --> 00:00:20,679
Each card shows the token, the window, and one number:

8
00:00:20,679 --> 00:00:22,225
how many sealed bids.

9
00:00:22,225 --> 00:00:24,800
Nothing about who bid, or how much.

10
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:33,314
To create a launch, you set the token name and symbol, the supply, the reserve price, the bidding window, and a cap per account.

11
00:00:33,314 --> 00:00:35,317
The preview updates as you type.

12
00:00:35,317 --> 00:00:38,135
Once it is live, nobody can change the rules.

13
00:00:38,135 --> 00:00:41,328
Here the demo wallet is on, so nothing is deployed.

14
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:44,020
Creating for real needs a signed-in wallet.

15
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:46,912
Now an open launch, and the bid form.

16
00:00:46,912 --> 00:00:48,851
Pick a limit price and a size.

17
00:00:48,851 --> 00:00:52,018
Optionally let an agent bid inside rules you set.

18
00:00:52,018 --> 00:00:56,413
Send refunds to another chain, with the address checked as you type.

19
00:00:56,413 --> 00:01:00,550
Or get a live price from NEAR Intents to pay from another chain.

20
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:05,980
The seal button needs a real signed-in wallet, so with the demo wallet it stays off.

21
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,274
Now bids from other buyers.

22
00:01:08,274 --> 00:01:12,658
Each one is sealed in their browser and sent through the real API.

23
00:01:12,658 --> 00:01:14,917
All you see is the count going up:

24
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:16,578
eleven, twelve, fourteen.

25
00:01:16,578 --> 00:01:19,700
Not who, not how much, until the window closes.

26
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,060
My bids shows what you locked, what you won, and what came back.

27
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:27,860
With the demo wallet this is sample data, and the page says so.

28
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:31,289
When the window closes, the creator settles.

29
00:01:31,289 --> 00:01:36,682
For this recording the wallet sign-in is skipped, and the same settle route runs.

30
00:01:36,682 --> 00:01:39,744
The engine opens the bids and finds one price:

31
00:01:39,744 --> 00:01:47,800
three cents for everyone, one million tokens sold, two bids left out with reasons, and a plan for where each refund goes.

32
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:49,077
Then verify.

33
00:01:49,077 --> 00:01:52,122
The page recomputes the result in your browser.

34
00:01:52,122 --> 00:01:53,223
Four checks pass.

35
00:01:53,223 --> 00:01:59,700
Two say not checked, the code and the hardware attestation, because we cannot check those from here.

36
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:02,189
The compare page is a playground.

37
00:02:02,189 --> 00:02:09,420
Add more bots, or let them bid higher, and watch who ends up with the tokens on a first-come launch and on a sealed one.

38
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:15,364
There is a how-it-works page, a page for creators, a FAQ, and an honest limits page:

39
00:02:15,364 --> 00:02:17,762
what this does, and what it does not.

40
00:02:17,762 --> 00:02:23,530
The enclave and the payment checks are stand-ins, nothing is attested, and no funds move.

41
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:25,269
That is the flow:

42
00:02:25,269 --> 00:02:29,351
create, bid, watch the sealed count, settle, and verify.

43
00:02:29,351 --> 00:02:30,080
Thank you.

